## 5. Exact API reference

### Everything you can import

```python
from pypods import (
    Game, Buttons, InputListener, Screen,            # classes
    run, key_tester, normalize_name, bar, clamp,     # functions
    PLUS, MINUS, MIDDLE, NEXT, PREV, HEADPHONE_BUTTONS, ANY,
    PRESS, RELEASE, TAP, DOUBLE_TAP, LONG_PRESS, EVENTS,
)
```

### Constants

| Name | Value / meaning |
|---|---|
| `PLUS` | `"plus"`: volume up; also keyboard `=` and `+` |
| `MINUS` | `"minus"`: volume down; also keyboard `-` and `_` |
| `MIDDLE` | `"middle"`: play/pause |
| `NEXT` | `"next"`: next track (not sent by every headset) |
| `PREV` | `"prev"`: previous track (not sent by every headset) |
| `HEADPHONE_BUTTONS` | tuple of all five above |
| `ANY` | use with `on()` to get every button |
| `PRESS`, `RELEASE`, `TAP`, `DOUBLE_TAP`, `LONG_PRESS` | `"press"`, `"release"`, `"tap"`, `"double_tap"`, `"long_press"` |
| `EVENTS` | all five event names together |

### Key names (for `controls` and `bind`)

- Letters and digits: `"a"`, `"w"`, `"7"` (lowercase; Shift doesn't matter)
- `"space"`, `"enter"`, `"esc"`, `"tab"`, `"backspace"`
- Arrows: `"up"`, `"down"`, `"left"`, `"right"`
- Modifiers: `"shift"`, `"ctrl_l"`, `"alt_l"`, `"cmd"`, and so on
- Function keys: `"f1"` to `"f12"`
- Unnamed keys: `"vk"` + code, e.g. `"vk96"`
- Aliases are accepted: `"Space"` / `" "` → `"space"`, `"escape"` → `"esc"`, `"return"` → `"enter"`, `"volume_up"` → `"plus"`. `normalize_name(name)` returns the official name.

**Warning:** `"="`, `"+"`, `"-"` and `"_"` already mean `plus` / `minus`. Don't bind them to other actions.

### Functions

| Function | What it does |
|---|---|
| `run(GameClass, **settings)` | creates the game, runs it, returns the game object. Also accepts an existing game object. Unknown setting names raise an error. |
| `key_tester(block_media_keys=False)` | interactive tool that prints every key and gesture |
| `normalize_name(name)` | official name of a key, e.g. `"="` → `"plus"` |
| `bar(value, maximum, width=20, fill="█", empty="░")` | text progress bar string |
| `clamp(value, low, high)` | limits a number to a range |

### Game: settings (class attributes, or keyword arguments to `run()`; `run()` wins)

| Setting | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `title` | `"Game"` | name of the game, for you to display |
| `fps` | `60` | frames per second; `None` = as fast as possible |
| `controls` | `{}` | `{action_name: [buttons...]}`; a single button may be a plain string |
| `block_media_keys` | `False` | Windows only: stop headphone buttons from also changing volume/music while the game runs. Prints a warning on other systems. |
| `block_volume_keys` | `False` | old name for `block_media_keys` |
| `double_tap_window` | `0.30` | seconds allowed between taps for a double tap |
| `long_press_time` | `0.50` | seconds to hold for a long press |
| `max_dt` | `0.25` | largest allowed `dt`; `None` turns the cap off |
| `listen` | `True` | `False` = don't read the keyboard (for tests or fed input) |

### Game: methods you write

| Method | When it runs |
|---|---|
| `setup(self)` | once, before the first frame |
| `update(self, dt)` | every frame; `dt` = seconds since last frame |
| `draw(self)` | every frame, after `update` |
| `teardown(self)` | once when the game stops, **even after a crash or Ctrl+C** |

### Game: read-only values

| Attribute | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `self.dt` | seconds since the last frame |
| `self.time` | seconds since the game loop started |
| `self.frame` | number of frames drawn |
| `self.running` | `True` while the loop runs |
| `self.buttons` | the `Buttons` object |
| `self.screen` | the `Screen` object used by `show()` |

### Game: controls

| Method | What it does |
|---|---|
| `self.bind(action, *buttons)` | adds buttons to an action (creates it if new) |
| `self.unbind(action, *buttons)` | removes those buttons from an action |
| `self.unbind(action)` | removes the whole action |
| `self.bindings(action)` | tuple of buttons for one action, e.g. `("plus", "space")` |
| `self.bindings()` | dict of every action and its buttons |

An action is held if **any** of its buttons is held.

### Game: input questions (use inside `update`)

| Method | Returns | True when |
|---|---|---|
| `self.action_held(a)` | bool | any button of the action is down now |
| `self.action_pressed(a)` | bool | a button went down this frame |
| `self.action_released(a)` | bool | a button came up this frame |
| `self.action_tapped(a)` | bool | a single short tap was confirmed this frame |
| `self.action_double_tapped(a)` | bool | a double tap happened this frame |
| `self.action_long_pressed(a)` | bool | a button just reached `long_press_time` while held |
| `self.action_held_for(a)` | float | seconds held so far, `0.0` if up |
| `self.action_axis(neg, pos)` | -1, 0, 1 | 1 if only `pos` held, -1 if only `neg`, 0 if both or neither |

Every question gives the same answer for the whole frame, however often you ask.

### Game: callbacks

| Method | What it does |
|---|---|
| `self.on_action(action, event, fn)` | call `fn` when `event` happens to any button of `action`; at most once per frame |
| `self.on(button, event, fn)` | same for one raw button; `event` defaults to `"press"` for both |
| `self.on(ANY, event, fn)` | call `fn` for every button |
| `self.buttons.off(button, event, fn)` | remove a callback added with `on` (omit `fn` to remove all for that button and event) |

- `event` is one of the five event strings; a typo raises an error.
- If you omit `event`, pass the function as a keyword: `self.on_action("jump", fn=self.jump)`.
- `fn` takes zero arguments, or one (the button name for `on`, the action name for `on_action`).
- Decorator form works: `@self.on_action("menu", DOUBLE_TAP)` above a function.
- Callbacks run on the main thread, during the next frame, **before** `update()`.
- Action callbacks look up bindings when the event happens, so `bind` order doesn't matter.

### Game: other

| Method | What it does |
|---|---|
| `self.show(text)` | draws text (multi-line OK), replacing what `show` drew last frame; skips redraw if unchanged |
| `self.quit()` | stops after the current frame; `teardown` runs; `run()` returns the game object |
| `self.run()` | starts the game (normally use the `run(MyGame)` function instead) |

### Buttons (`self.buttons`, or create `Buttons()` yourself)

| Member | What it does |
|---|---|
| `held(name)`, `pressed(name)`, `released(name)` | raw-button versions of the action questions |
| `tapped(name)`, `double_tapped(name)`, `long_pressed(name)` | gestures for one button |
| `held_for(name)` | seconds held, `0.0` if up |
| `axis(negative, positive)` | -1 / 0 / 1 from two buttons, e.g. `axis(MINUS, PLUS)` |
| `any_pressed()` | name of a button pressed this frame, or `None` |
| `all_held()` | set of every button held now |
| `on(name, event, fn)` / `off(name, event, fn)` | add / remove callbacks |
| `press(name)`, `release(name)`, `tap(name)` | fake input; appears on the next frame |
| `feed(name, is_down)` | low-level fake input; safe from any thread |
| `clear()` | forget held buttons and half-finished gestures |
| `update()` | advance one frame (only call it yourself when not using `Game`) |
| `frame` | number of `update()` calls |
| `double_tap_window`, `long_press_time` | gesture timings, changeable any time |

### InputListener

| Member | What it does |
|---|---|
| `InputListener(buttons, block_media_keys=False)` | create a listener that feeds a `Buttons` object |
| `.start()` / `.stop()` | start / stop; `start()` returns the listener |
| `with InputListener(buttons): ...` | starts on enter, always stops on exit |
| `.running` | `True` while listening |
| `.key_name(key)` | pynput key object → pypods name |

### Screen

| Member | What it does |
|---|---|
| `Screen()` or `Screen(some_file)` | terminal drawer (the `Game` makes one for you) |
| `.show(text)` | same as `Game.show` |
| `.finish()` | moves the cursor below the drawn text |

